-- title:
--   Kernel degree of a sandwiched mathcal O_D-homomorphism is an even power of r
-- statement:
--   Let $r$ be a prime and $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $r$, and let $j : \mathbb Z_{r^2} \to k$ be a ring homomorphism, where $\mathbb Z_{r^2}$ is realised as the Witt vectors of the field with $r^2$ elements. Let $Y$ and $Y'$ be formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over $k$, i.e. each consists of a commutative two-dimensional formal group law over $k$ together with an action of $\mathbb Z_{r^2}$ by endomorphisms of the law and a uniformiser series $\varpi$, subject to the usual compatibilities ($\varpi \circ \varpi = [r]$ and $\varpi \circ [a] = [\mathrm{Frob}\,a] \circ \varpi$). Assume both are special for $j$, that is, the $j$-eigenspace and the $j\circ\mathrm{Frob}$-eigenspace of the $\mathbb Z_{r^2}$-action on the Lie module are complementary and each invertible, and that both have height $4$, meaning that $[r]$ has kernel degree $r^4$. Let $\sigma$ be a pair of power series in two variables which is an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism from $Y$ to $Y'$ (a homomorphism of formal group laws commuting with the $\mathbb Z_{r^2}$-action and with $\varpi$), and let $\tau$ be a pair of power series with zero constant terms such that $\tau \circ \sigma = [r^d]_Y$ for some $d \in \mathbb N$. Then there is $m \in \mathbb N$ with `HasKernelOfDegree` $\sigma\ (r^{2m})$: the quotient of $k[[X_1,X_2]]$ by the ideal generated by the two components of $\sigma$ is a finite projective $k$-module, and for every field $\kappa$ and ring homomorphism $k \to \kappa$ the corresponding quotient over $\kappa$ has $\kappa$-dimension $r^{2m}$.
--
--   This is the field-point kernel-degree statement for $\mathcal O_D$-isogenies between special formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules of height $4$: an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism admitting a one-sided inverse up to $[r^d]$ is an isogeny whose kernel has degree an even power of $r$. It is used in the rigidification step for fake elliptic curves on the Čerednik–Drinfeld route, where it makes the parity of the transported isogeny well defined.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_exists_hasKernelOfDegree_pow_two_mul_of_isODHom_of_comp_eq_act_pow_of_isAlgClosed.lean

